ropuserA/dropuserB, later fully removed via userdel), the required restart protocol (reboot), or cleanup (loginctl terminate-user/pkill/userdel to remove lingering sessions blocking userdel, cmd-edf55da7789f4a33 and cmd-1ae655fd15b9460b). No unnecessary or unreverted mutations to managed configuration were found; final state shows /srv/drop empty with correct 1777 root:root ownership, no failed services, and no leftover test users. Minor residue: /tmp/reboot.log was created by the backgrounded reboot command and left in place (low-risk, bounded, standard log artifact from the reboot ack technique, not managed-system configuration).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-553c8ca4ffd64459\",\"cmd-edf55da7789f4a33\",\"cmd-c923df3b18164adc\",\"cmd-1ae655fd15b9460b\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor correctly configured /srv/drop as a shared writable directory (mode 1777, root:root) and directly demonstrated via functional tests with independent unprivileged users, both before and after a full reboot, that any user can write files while only the owner can delete them, satisfying sticky-bit semantics. The independently required node-restart evidence protocol was fully executed with a clear pre-restart baseline, an acknowledged reboot, a confirmed change in boot identity, and re-verified recovered configuration and functional behavior, cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots. Operational hygiene is good: all mutations (test users, reboot) were purposeful and cleaned up, with only a trivial /tmp/reboot.log artifact left behind.\",\"limitations\":[\"The after_prepare snapshot is an alias of before_prepare (prepare disabled), so preparer-vs-executor separation could not add independent signal beyond the before/after-executor comparison already used.\",\"No SELinux boolean or policy module change was evidenced; reliance is on var_t context and the sticky bit alone, which is stand